Let's start with TRIM. Unlike regular hard drives, SSDs cannot detect on their
own which blocks are no longer in use after files are deleted. The file system
only updates its metadata and does not inform the drive that those blocks are
free. To address this, the TRIM command was introduced: it explicitly tells the
SSD which blocks can be reused. Without TRIM, the SSD must perform additional
internal cleanup (garbage collection) when space is needed, which can cause
write performance to degrade once the drive has been filled at least once.

For this reason, it is recommended to periodically run a TRIM operation on all
disks that support it. This informs the SSD about freed space. btrfs issues
asynchronous TRIM commands by default, so no extra configuration is required.
However, since there is at least one FAT32 partition on our system (the EFI
system partition) and you may have additional partitions with other file systems
that do not automatically discard, it is still advisable to activate systemd’s
fstrim.timer. This performs a weekly TRIM on all supported devices.

If your system does not include an SSD, there is no need to activate this
service.